<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jehoash of Israel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 798–782 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Joash of Israel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jehoash, or Joash, was king of Israel in the early eighth century BCE, the grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a>. Taking advantage of Assyrian pressure on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, he defeated the Arameans three times and recovered towns lost by his father, and he defeated and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amaziah-of-judah">Amaziah of Judah</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beth-shemesh">Beth-shemesh</a> and plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. A stele of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adad-nirari-iii">Adad-nirari III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> records his tribute.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jehoash-of-israel.jpg" alt="Jehoash of Israel" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guillaume Rouille · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jehoash_of_Israel.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
